# Chapter 6 — Ontological Uncertainty

*What is left when we remove everything we already know how to name?*

The engine inherits a physical world. Its materials have properties, its possible transformations have constraints, and its operation draws on differences it did not create. The previous chapter asked how an existing productive system can support the construction of a new pathway. We now turn to a deeper question: how could the conditions of physical existence themselves emerge?

This chapter and the next two separate questions that need different kinds of answers. This chapter examines ontological uncertainty: indeterminacy proposed as a constituent of reality. Chapter 7 investigates symmetry breaking and disequilibrium through physical mechanisms. Chapter 8 asks whether laws and constants could have a material history, and what that would mean for fine-tuning. Together they explore cosmic emergence as a natural process. They do not assume a uniquely required outcome.

The joint material and Platonic claim becomes especially demanding here. In engineering, we can distinguish a design from its realization while inheriting the laws that make realization possible. At the proposed origin of those laws, the range of possible designs can no longer be treated as an unexplained catalog outside the world. What supplies the formal possibilities? What makes a transition between them physically meaningful? If assembly produces existence, what does assembly presuppose?

The difficulty becomes sharper when we ask what preceded the order we know. It is tempting to begin with nothing: no stars, no particles, no space, and then some event by which existence arrives. But that sentence has already smuggled in an event and a relation of before and after. We have described nothing by giving it the conditions under which something might happen.

Emptying a container does not escape the problem. Remove the air and the container remains. Remove the container and we still imagine a place. Remove particles from a physical theory and the theory may retain fields, possible states, relations, and laws. Each removal takes away something within a description. It does not necessarily leave absolute nothingness.

A quantum vacuum belongs on this side of the distinction. In the elementary field-theory setting, it is a lowest-energy state, with properties defined by the theory. It can have nontrivial fluctuations and correlations even though it contains no particles of the specified free-field kinds. These statements concern an organized mathematical and physical structure. They do not describe a featureless absence from which the structure has somehow been obtained.[1]

The ontological starting point proposed here is explicit: absolute nothingness does not and cannot exist. Uncertainty is fundamental to existence. It names an indeterminacy constituting physical reality, including the conditions from which definite structures emerge. This is a claim about what the world is, not just about what an observer can know.

We can be uncertain because information is missing. A coin covered by a hand may already show heads or tails. Statistical descriptions can also summarize variation or assign probabilities to outcomes. The uncertainty proposed as fundamental here is neither missing knowledge nor a probability distribution. A distribution might describe some consequences of an indeterminate reality; the ontological claim concerns the reality that makes such outcomes possible.

In this account, reality and physical structure emerge from underlying uncertainty. Relations become sufficiently definite to support enduring configurations, while the conditions of further formation remain part of the same process. This is where the material and Platonic argument reaches its foundation: physical realization and organizing relations would emerge together, with each achieved structure changing the possibilities for subsequent structures. The proposed uncertainty is constitutive of that becoming.

The proposed account seeks an internal explanation of emergence. It does not require a prior stock of completely formed objects, an external initiator, or an independently completed plan. Indeterminacy supplies its starting proposal; a generative mechanism must still explain how definite relations and physical structures form. Describing emergence as a natural process makes that mechanism the object of inquiry.

The author proposes that absolute nothingness would be a wholly determinate absence of possibilities, relations, and events, incompatible with constitutive indeterminacy. This is a philosophical premise to defend. It cannot simply be converted into a physical derivation: declaring absence impossible does not explain which structures form, how they form, or why this universe has its observed properties.

Continual becoming names the proposed relationship between existence and the production of structure. It does not establish that our universe had to occur or that its past is infinitely long. If time is itself assembled, the account must explain the ordering of this process without borrowing an external clock. The claim pursued here is natural emergence, with the particular history and its contingencies still to be explained.[2]

Quantum mechanics supplies a precise account of some physical uncertainties, but it does not settle this ontological proposal simply by containing an uncertainty relation. Relations such as the position–momentum uncertainty bound constrain the spreads of measurement outcomes associated with a quantum state. They are not merely instructions to buy more accurate instruments. At the same time, interpretations of quantum theory differ about the underlying ontology. Bohm's deterministic formulation is one reason not to equate quantum predictions automatically with a unique metaphysics of fundamental chance.[3]

The proposal that existence emerges from uncertainty therefore needs more than a reference to Heisenberg. It needs to explain what is indeterminate, how that indeterminacy is represented, and why it yields actuality and physical structure. Excluding a completely determined reality would not, without the connecting premise, establish the impossibility of nothingness. Nor would it specify a cosmos, its laws, or its history. Those are further steps the proposed ontology must justify.

The book proposes that indeterminacy is fundamental and that physical organization emerges through natural processes. Its rejection of absolute nothingness is an ontological commitment, not an empirical finding about the beginning of the universe. A physical development of the proposal must derive observable consequences and explain any connection to disequilibrium and the proposed entropy-production tendency. Ontological indeterminacy is not itself a thermodynamic gradient or a rate law.

The vacuum has structure. That fact makes the vacuum interesting; it does not make it nothing.

An explanation must connect the possible to the actual. If indeterminacy means that several outcomes are permitted, the account still needs relations through which an outcome becomes physically definite. If it means that the space of possibilities itself develops, the account must specify how that development can be represented. Calling both material and formal organization assembled sets a research problem; it does not discharge it.

The evidentiary task is correspondingly limited. We cannot compare an observed universe with an observed state of absolute nothingness. No natural experiment currently identifies the ontology proposed here. A physical model might nevertheless acquire empirical content by deriving an observable relation that competing models do not predict. Until that bridge is supplied, the philosophical proposal must be assessed through its premises and coherence, while the physical predictions remain to be developed.[4]

The next chapter begins where an explicit state space and interactions are already available. It asks how a balanced arrangement can become differentiated and how differences can drive further change. That move makes part of the problem experimentally tractable while leaving the ontological foundation open.

