2. **Ontological uncertainty and continual becoming.** I68 records the author's claim that absolute nothingness is impossible, uncertainty constitutes existence, and the universe needs no additional cause to begin. The connection between indeterminacy and continual becoming is the book's philosophical proposal; it does not derive a necessary cosmic outcome. [S20](sources.md#s20) addresses quantum uncertainty and differing ontologies; [S21](sources.md#s21) describes the structured quantum vacuum. Neither source proves this ontological conclusion or an infinitely extended past. The afterword identifies the connecting premises, temporal interpretation, and empirical consequences still needed.

3. H. P. Robertson, [“The Uncertainty Principle”](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.34.163) (1929); David Bohm, [“A Suggested Interpretation of the Quantum Theory in Terms of ‘Hidden’ Variables. I”](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.85.166) (1952). These distinguish formal uncertainty relations from a single compulsory interpretation of quantum ontology.

